If you are carrying something the pet considers to
be a treat, the pet also tends to hover closer to
you(*), so since the pet is constantly picking stuff
up and dropping it anyway, purely at random, a tripe
ration, over the long run, might net you more
"apport" behavior from a carnivorous pet by having
the ration as part of your knapsack than by feeding
it to your pet to give the apport variable a one
time boost, even though the "apport" variable is not
directly affected by carried treats. This
"apport"-like behavior would really be just from the
increase in stuff randomly dropped by a pet which is
more often right next to you. It helps, in this
case, to put stuff in the shop you want stolen, two
steps straight inside the door before you step into
the doorway, because a pet hovering close to you is
also stepping on nearby items more often, increasing
the odds of picking them up.

It's a puzzlement which way to play that one
precious treat, but then so is much of the rest of
NetHack. Even when you read the code, something
seemingly unrelated and far away can make what you
think you understand only a modest and
unrepresentative part of the whole story.

xanthian.

(*) This makes having a tripe ration and a sack a
good combination for "herding cats" in Sokoban. Bag
the ration when you want the pet to wander off to
keep the pet out of your way while pushing boulders,
unbag the ration when trying to get the pet next to
you for going up or down stairs.  You merely change
odds of a behavior (moving toward the PC), not whole
behaviors in either case, but that's often all the
help you need. Works for me!



